ue in as-hot-rolled material\",\"explanation\":\"GRAIN-STRUCTURE INHOMOGENEITY is described as nonuniformity in γ- and α-grain structures that remains characteristic of as-hot-rolled material. The data identify reduction of recrystallized γ-grain size before deformation as a means of reducing this inhomogeneity. [Data: Entities (511); Relationships (665)]\\n\\nAS-HOT-ROLLED MATERIAL is directly connected to grain-structure inhomogeneity, with the relationship describing inhomogeneity as part of the material’s resulting condition. This positions structural uniformity as a central quality consideration within the community. [Data: Entities (512); Relationships (664)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Mixed α-grain structures may reduce toughness\",\"explanation\":\"The α-grain structure description states that recrystallization or partial recrystallization during holding can produce a mixed α-grain structure. The same description indicates that such a mixed structure may reduce toughness. [Data: Entities (170)]\\n\\nThis finding highlights a potential tradeoff in process control: achieving fine and homogeneous α grains is important not only for structural refinement but also for preserving the toughness associated with a more favorable microstructure. No quantitative toughness data or operating thresholds are provided. [Data: Entities (170)]\"},{\"summary\":\"The community represents an interconnected controlled-rolling process model\",\"explanation\":\"The relationships form a process-oriented structure: deformation amount and recrystallized γ-grain size influence α-grain structure; deformation-band uniformity influences α-nucleation uniformity; and as-hot-rolled material is associated with grain-structure inhomogeneity. [Data: Relationships (266, 216, 269, 270, 664, 665)]\\n\\nThe available evidence supports technical conclusions about microstructural development and material properties, but it does not provide information about legal compliance, organizational ownership, reputatio